d after this."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "The 20k's E2 had just handed the lead back at +2.7. D3 then lost 8.0 points and sent it to -5.3. The engine again wanted G8. A good chance went begging twice in three turns."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "H7 turned a game that was still close at -2.4 into -9.7, a loss of 7.3 points. The engine's choice was D9. This was the start of a run of moves on the right side that each cost heavily."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "J7 lost 8.3 points and took the lead to -16.1, the lowest point of the game. The engine preferred C8, which GPT-6.1 Sol only played on turn 41, when it was worth nothing."}, {"turn": 34, "text": "The 20k's J6 lost 8.3 points and gave the lead back to -7.8. It still left Black far behind. After this, the game kept sliding by smaller amounts until Black's top-right stones were gone and White held the whole ninth row."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything the code ran was inspection or record-keeping: the inspection.js board parser/group/liberty/area counter, a one-time fixed-region target count after move 10, verification.js replaying the already-played moves, plus play/board calls and file writes. Nothing looped over empty points to score candidates or looked ahead, so move choices were manual.", "stop": false}}